<figure class="catch-up" aria-labelledby="catch-up-title">
  <figcaption id="catch-up-title"><span>Figure 4</span> Catching up needs capacity beyond new writes</figcaption>
  <div class="model">
    <p class="backlog"><span>Existing backlog</span><strong>600 MiB</strong><small>History still to replay</small></p>
    <div class="cases">
      <div><p>Writes continue</p><div class="rate"><span style="width:100%">Replay: 20 MiB/s</span></div><div class="rate"><span class="incoming" style="width:50%">New: 10 MiB/s</span></div><p class="calculation">Net drain: 20 − 10 = 10 MiB/s</p><strong>600 ÷ 10 = 60 s</strong></div>
      <div><p>Writes stop</p><div class="rate"><span style="width:100%">Replay: 20 MiB/s</span></div><div class="rate empty">No new log: 0 MiB/s</div><p class="calculation">Net drain: 20 − 0 = 20 MiB/s</p><strong>600 ÷ 20 = 30 s</strong></div>
    </div>
    <p class="limit">If replay and new log both run at 20 MiB/s, the backlog stays at 600 MiB.</p>
  </div>
  <p class="caption">An arithmetic model with assumed, constant rates and no other bottleneck. The bars compare log rates, not transaction counts. These are ideal estimates, not observed catch-up times or deadlines.</p>
</figure>
